The most common and important special case is when the two spaces are the same. Thus, if \( (S, \mathscr S, \lambda) \) is a measure space and \( k : S \times S \to [0, \infty) \) is measurable, then we have an operator \( K \) that operates on the left and on the right with measurable functions \( f: S \to \R \):
\begin{align*} 
	f K(y) & = \int_S \lambda(dx) f(x) k(x, y), \quad y \in S \\
	K f(x) & = \int_S k(x, y) f(y) \lambda(d y), \quad x \in S
\end{align*}
If \( f \) is nonnegative and \( \mu \) is the measure on with density function \( f \), then \( f K \) is the density function of the measure \( \mu K \) (both with respect to \( \lambda \)).

